\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{The square Dirichlet form and sampled fields}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{Fix #{m_0>0}, #{\ell>0} and an odd integer #{N\ge5}. Put
##{\begin{aligned}
\Lambda_\ell&=(-\ell /2,\ell /2)^2,&\epsilon&=\frac{\ell}{N+1},\\
V_{\ell,N}&=\Lambda_\ell\cap\epsilon\mathbb Z^2,&
H_{\ell,N}&=L^2(V_{\ell,N},\epsilon^2\text{ counting};\mathbb R).
\end{aligned}}
The set #{V_{\ell,N}} has #{N^2} points. Write #{h_\epsilon} for the
Hilbert pairing and #{j} for extension by zero to the whole lattice.
Let #{E} contain each undirected nearest-neighbor bond with at least one
endpoint in #{V_{\ell,N}} exactly once, and choose an orientation for
each bond. The incidence map #{d_D:H_{\ell,N}\to\mathbb R^E} sends
#{\phi} to the oriented differences of #{j\phi}; the edge space has its
counting pairing.}

\p{The \newvocab{square Dirichlet form} and its Riesz operator are
##{\begin{aligned}
\mathfrak q_{\epsilon,D}(\phi,\psi)
 &=m_0^2h_\epsilon(\phi,\psi)
     +\langle d_D\phi,d_D\psi\rangle_E,\\
Q_{\epsilon,D}&=m_0^2I+d_D^*d_D,\qquad
\mathfrak q_{\epsilon,D}(\phi,\psi)
 =h_\epsilon(\phi,Q_{\epsilon,D}\psi).
\end{aligned}}
Here the adjoint uses the declared vertex and edge metrics. The
\vocabk{Gaussian probability law}{fdip-0005} #{\mu_{Q_{\epsilon,D}}}
has covariance operator #{Q_{\epsilon,D}^{-1}} on #{H_{\ell,N}}.
For a test function #{f}, let #{R_\epsilon f=f|_{V_{\ell,N}}} be the
restriction map. The \newvocab{point-sampled field} is the linear map
from tests to linear observables
##{\Phi_\epsilon(f):\phi\longmapsto
             h_\epsilon(R_\epsilon f,\phi).}
Initially #{f\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb R^2;\mathbb R)}.}

\p{This is the killed-lattice construction and point-sampling
convention of \citet{(IV.3)-(IV.8), pp. 192-193; Theorem IV.9,
p. 202}{guerra1975euclideanII}. The metric identifies the energy form
with its positive operator; its coordinate expression is calculated in
\ref{fdip-00HV}.}
